h `--plugin-dir`, sets `VIOLA_BIN` to the copy and puts the copy's folder first on the child's PATH. `hooks.json` registers seven exec-form entries, each `"command": "@@VIOLA_BIN@@"` (substituted at start) with `"args": ["hook", "<kebab event>"]`: SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit and Stop with `"timeout": 5`; SessionEnd with no `timeout` key; Notification, PostToolUse and PostToolUseFailure with `"async": true`. The dialog tier joins them (chunk 2026-10-04-dialog-answers-by-dialog-id): PreToolUse with `"matcher": "AskUserQuestion|ExitPlanMode"` and PermissionRequest, each with `"timeout": 75` — nine exec-form entries in all. `.mcp.json` registers the server `viola` as `{"mcpServers": {"viola": {"command": "@@VIOLA_BIN@@", "args": ["mcp"]}}}`, the pinned copy's absolute path substituted at every start (chunk 2026-10-17-mcp-server-for-drivers; until the `mcp` verb existed it shipped `{"mcpServers": {}}`, on the operator ruling at the 2026-09-27-instance-state-and-start-order plan that a registered command that exits 2 would block Claude Code). Whether a registered `viola mcp` that exits 1 blocks the CLI's start is unmeasured, owed to the route entry "Compact post-condition ledger row". Hooks and MCP never search PATH. Rationale: the binary and the plugin can no longer drift apart. The prototype proved that PATH-found hooks run a different binary from the wrapper after a rebuild. The content hash is part of the key because `CARGO_PKG_VERSION` stays `0.1.0` across development rebuilds, so the version alone would collide. A rebuild therefore never changes what a running session calls. Known trade-off: a hook fix applies only to sessions started after the rebuild. The per-frame `v` + sender version stays as the second safety net, for the optional user-level install (`viola plugin install`), which is not bound to a wrapper's pinned copy.
+- **[Plugin Scope] The plugin loads only into wrapped sessions via `--plugin-dir`.** Both driver and driven run under `viola run`. `viola plugin install` (a local marketplace) is optional, for the MCP verbs in an unwrapped driver. The installed plugin carries only `.mcp.json` and no hooks, so a wrapped session that also has it installed never runs two hook processes for one event (two `hook.dialog` requests for one dialog). Its `command` is the absolute path of the exe that ran `plugin install`, never a PATH lookup: as built, `viola plugin install` writes only `<home>/marketplace/` and spawns no `claude`, and its `.mcp.json` registers `viola` as `{"command": <the canonical current_exe, forward slashes>, "args": ["--home", <the canonical home, forward slashes>, "mcp"]}`, each document built by `serde_json` (`viola_agent_claude::marketplace::marketplace_files`). Whether Claude Code loads both a `--plugin-dir` plugin and an installed plugin with the same name, and which `viola` MCP server a wrapped session then starts and uses, is no capability-ledger row (no `send` relies on it, the ruled limit) and is not measured: owed to the route entry "Compact post-condition ledger row", its Windows leg to "Windows-only live measurements". Rationale: unwrapped sessions carry no viola hooks at all, so there is no per-tool-call spawn cost outside viola. The exceptions are `viola verify`'s transient unwrapped children, all gone when verify ends. The first is the print-mode probe, an unwrapped `claude -p` that loads only the `viola-verify-probe` plugin (four exec-form `hook <event> --capture` hooks) from `ledger/probes/<pid>/plugin/`. The other five are the interactive runs: Run A, untrusted in an OS-temp `viola-verify-*` dir and killed at its screen; Run B, trusted in `<cwd>/.viola-verify-<pid>/`; Runs C and D, trusted in `<cwd>/.viola-verify-<pid>-dialogs/` and `<cwd>/.viola-verify-<pid>-plan/`, each loading the dialog-kind `viola-verify-probe` plugin; and Run E, trusted in `<cwd>/.viola-verify-<pid>-resume/`, loading the capture plugin from `ledger/probes/<pid>/resume/plugin/` and resuming Run B's first session with `--resume <id>`, no key typed. Runs B, C and D, as real interactive turns, also run the user's own global hooks, and Runs C and D, and Run B on Windows, the user's own status line (on Unix Run B's status line is the probe capture its one probe `--settings` names, [CLI Version Compatibility]), and each leaves the CLI's session transcripts under `~/.claude/projects/` (an accepted residual, security-plan Data Protection): Run B two per real-CLI verify, its session and the one its pasted `/clear` opens, and C and D one each; Run E adds none (as measured on 2.1.287 at chunk 2026-10-14-resume-and-compact-ledger-rows, `evidence/live-round.md`).
 - **[CLI Conventions] Human text by default, `--json` for agents, a typed exit code per refusal. Prompt text comes from stdin or `--file`, never from a leading-slash argument.** viola warns when an argument carries a Git Bash rewritten-path prefix. Rationale: Git Bash rewrites `/skill` arguments into Windows paths (brief §6), and agents need machine-parseable refusals. `viola revive` takes `--json` on both arms (chunk 2026-10-17-cli-machine-contract): a refused strict-modes check or a preflight refusal prints `{"v":1,"error":"<code>","detail":null}` (`strict-modes-failed` · `already-live` · `no-session` · `session-live` · `cwd-missing`) in place of its `unable:` / `hint:` pair, exit 1, and `<name> --list --json` prints `{"v":1,"ok":{"sessions":[…]}}` in log order; without `--json` the pairs and the `--list` rows are byte-identical. A refusal `run`'s start makes after a passed preflight (`batch-script-child`, `pinned-hash-mismatch`, `squatted-name`, a `claude` not on `PATH` as `internal-error`) prints no document as landed; its document is owed to the working-route entry "Exit-cause code catalogue".
